"""State-level 'Local costs and rules' content for the 20 city pages (Oct 2026 audit).
Replaced an empty heading left when the draft note was stripped. Only statements we could verify
against primary sources: Claims Journal 2025 hail payouts (TX $1.4B, then MO and IL), Texas Insurance
Code ch. 707 and Colorado C.R.S. 10-4-110.8 on deductible rebating, Florida's 2023 assignment-of-
benefits changes. Everything else is stated as general practice, not local pricing."""
from common import esc

CLAIMS = "https://www.claimsjournal.com/news/national/2026/04/23/337102.htm"

STATE = {
    "Texas": f"""<p>Texas led the country in hail claim payouts in 2025, with about $1.4 billion paid by State Farm alone (<a href="{CLAIMS}" rel="noopener">Claims Journal</a>). That volume shapes everything about financing a roof here. Most Texas homeowners policies now carry a separate wind and hail deductible set as a percentage of the dwelling coverage, commonly 1% or 2%, so the out-of-pocket on a $350,000 home can be $3,500 to $7,000 before uncovered items. Texas law (Insurance Code chapter 707) makes it illegal for a contractor to waive, rebate or absorb that deductible, and insurers can require proof you paid it before releasing the final claim payment. Financing the deductible is legal and common; "free roof" offers are not.</p>""",
    "Oklahoma": """<p>Oklahoma sits in the heart of the hail and tornado belt, and roofs here are replaced on a storm cycle rather than an age cycle. Percentage wind and hail deductibles are common on newer policies, and insurers increasingly pay older roofs on a depreciated or scheduled basis. Ask your agent how your roof is valued before storm season. When a claim pays most of the roof, the financing question is usually the deductible plus items the adjuster didn't allow, which is a small, short loan rather than a full roof loan.</p>""",
    "Colorado": """<p>Colorado's Front Range is one of the most hail-exposed corridors in the country, and Colorado law (C.R.S. 10-4-110.8) prohibits roofing contractors from paying, waiving or rebating a homeowner's insurance deductible. The same statute gives homeowners the right to cancel a roofing contract if the claim is denied. Impact-resistant shingles are popular here because many insurers offer premium discounts for them; ask your agent before choosing materials, since the discount can offset part of the higher cost over the roof's life. Percentage deductibles are common on wind and hail coverage.</p>""",
    "Missouri": f"""<p>Missouri was among the top states for hail claim payouts in 2025, behind only Texas in State Farm's figures (<a href="{CLAIMS}" rel="noopener">Claims Journal</a>). Spring storms drive most replacements, and policies increasingly use percentage wind and hail deductibles and roof age schedules that reduce what the insurer pays on older roofs. Get the adjuster's estimate and a roofer's itemized quote side by side before deciding how much to finance; the gap is often smaller than homeowners fear once depreciation is released at completion.</p>""",
    "Kansas": """<p>Kansas roofs face hail from the south and wind from every direction, and most replacements here follow a claim. Percentage wind and hail deductibles are standard on many policies, and some insurers now pay roofs on an age schedule. Because replacement-cost policies hold back depreciation until the work is done, many homeowners need short-term financing to bridge the roofer's payment until the second check arrives, and a smaller permanent loan for the deductible and uncovered items.</p>""",
    "Nebraska": """<p>Nebraska's hail season runs from spring through summer, and insurers have responded with higher wind and hail deductibles and closer scrutiny of roof age. Replacement-cost policies typically pay in two parts, with depreciation released after completion. Homeowners here commonly finance the deductible and bridge the depreciation gap rather than the whole roof. Energy-efficiency rebates from local utilities can reduce what you finance on HVAC replacements, which are often bundled with roof work after storm damage.</p>""",
    "Indiana": """<p>Indiana roofs take hail in spring and ice and snow load in winter, so replacements come from both storm claims and plain age. For claims, check whether your policy pays replacement cost or actual cash value on the roof and whether a percentage wind and hail deductible applies. For age-driven replacements with no claim, the financing is the full job, so the choice between an unsecured loan, a contractor plan and a home equity product matters more. Utility rebates for high-efficiency furnaces and heat pumps are worth checking before financing HVAC work.</p>""",
    "Minnesota": """<p>Minnesota homeowners deal with summer hail and winter ice dams, and both lead to roof claims. Minnesota has specific consumer protections around storm-chasing roofers and contracts signed after a storm, so read any post-storm agreement carefully and don't sign anything contingent on the insurer's approval without understanding the cancellation terms. Cold-climate heat pumps and high-efficiency furnaces often qualify for utility rebates here, which reduce the amount you finance.</p>""",
    "Florida": """<p>Florida is a hurricane market, and that changes the math. Hurricane deductibles are almost always a percentage of dwelling coverage, often 2% or more, and the policy may apply them per season. Florida reformed assignment-of-benefits agreements for policies issued or renewed from 2023, so most homeowners now handle the claim themselves rather than signing it over to a contractor; read anything a contractor asks you to sign very carefully. Roof age and condition heavily affect whether a home can be insured at all in Florida, which makes planned replacement, and financing it, part of keeping coverage. Permits and wind-mitigation inspections are standard parts of a Florida re-roof.</p>""",
    "Tennessee": """<p>Tennessee sees severe spring storms, tornadoes and hail, with claims concentrated in a few bad weeks each year. Percentage wind and hail deductibles are increasingly common on newer policies. Because storm damage tends to be widespread, reputable local roofers book up fast after an event; having financing options sorted early can keep you from accepting the first door-knocker. For planned replacements without a claim, compare an unsecured loan with a contractor plan and, if you have equity and time, a home equity product.</p>""",
    "Utah": """<p>Utah roofs see less hail than the Plains but take heavy snow loads, intense sun and wind, and many replacements here are age-driven rather than claim-driven. That means financing the full job, so comparing total cost across an unsecured loan, a contractor plan and a home equity product matters more than in storm markets. Utah's dry climate makes high-efficiency AC and evaporative-to-refrigerated conversions common HVAC projects, and local utility rebates can reduce what you finance.</p>""",
}

COMMON = """<p>The same lenders fund the rest of the house. If the project is a kitchen, a fence, a generator, siding, windows or a water heater rather than a roof, our <a href="/home-improvement-financing/">home improvement financing</a> page covers every project type, and <a href="/hvac-financing/">HVAC financing</a> covers AC, furnace and heat pump replacements.</p><p>Two things apply everywhere. First, roof prices vary with size, pitch, layers to remove, material and local labor, so two or three itemized quotes on the same scope tell you more than any average. Second, check the contractor before the loan: a current state license or registration where one exists, proof of insurance, a local address and reviews that predate the storm. The Federal Trade Commission's <a href="https://consumer.ftc.gov/articles/hiring-contractor" rel="noopener">guide to hiring a contractor</a> lists the warning signs. Our <a href="/roof-financing/">roof financing</a> page compares the loan types, and our guide to <a href="/blog/roof-financing-after-hail-damage/">roof financing after hail damage</a> walks through a claim step by step.</p>"""

def local_section(state, city):
    return STATE.get(state, "") + COMMON.replace("Two things apply everywhere", f"Two things apply to every {esc(city)} roof")
